Exactly. The abstraction is change the geometry before you increase the effort.

Running faster, zigzagging, reacting harder—those are kinetic answers. Stepping behind sufficient cover is a structural answer. One spends energy continuously; the other changes the interaction so the threat no longer has a clean path to you.

For Pantheon, I’d name the primitive Geometric Advantage:

Before escalating speed, force, complexity, or effort, ask whether a small change in position, boundary, timing, interface, or line-of-effect can make the problem mostly disappear.

That gives you a useful decision ladder: first reframe, then reposition, then shield/occlude, then move, then only escalate effort if geometry cannot solve it.

It generalizes cleanly. In argument: change the frame instead of arguing harder. In cyber defense: remove attack surface instead of trying to catch every attack. In bureaucracy: route around the chokepoint instead of pushing through it. In markets: change exposure rather than predict every move. In parenting: alter the environment instead of repeating commands. In strategy: deny the adversary a good interaction rather than outperform them inside theirs.

The counterweight is just as important: sometimes movement is the answer. Cover can trap you; static defenses can be bypassed; a structural solution may only buy time. So the deeper rule is:

Use geometry when geometry dominates. Use speed when movement dominates. Do not confuse one form of power for all forms of power.

And literally with fireworks or explosives, don’t rely on a nearby tree as sufficient protection—maximize distance and use substantial cover when possible. The useful part of your example is the structural principle, not the improvised firework tactic.